Add a bounded 16 KiB recent-log ring + GET /api/logs to the Wi-Fi
variants' local trusted-LAN HTTP server. Applied only to
nanoc6-wifi and atoms3-lite-wifi via variants.json;
nanoc6-thread never applies, compiles, links, or starts any of it.

Scope, keep minimal (no log viewer UI, no OTA, no WebSocket, no
authentication, no settings changes):

  * MODIFY main/aliro_local_web.h: declare
      esp_err_t AliroLocalWebLogInit(void);
    which app_main calls exactly once at boot to install the log
    sink. The ring lock is a statically initialised portMUX with
    no runtime construction, so this entry point always returns
    ESP_OK.

  * MODIFY main/aliro_local_web.cpp: add a static 16 KiB ring
    (s_log_ring), a portMUX spinlock (s_log_lock,
    portMUX_INITIALIZER_UNLOCKED — statically initialised), a
    kLogLineMax = 256 stack format buffer, and:
      - AliroLocalWebLogInit: holds s_log_lock across the
        installed check, the esp_log_set_vprintf exchange, the
        publication of s_prev_vprintf, and the publication of
        s_log_installed, so two concurrent callers see exactly
        one exchange and the callback never observes a
        half-published state. esp_log_set_vprintf is safe to
        call inside a portMUX critical section because the IDF
        entry point is a single __atomic_exchange_n(...,
        SEQ_CST) with no internal mutex. The return of the
        exchange is refused if it points at log_sink_vprintf so
        an external re-install cannot make us capture our own
        callback as the prior sink. Always returns ESP_OK.
      - log_sink_vprintf: reentrant. First takes s_log_lock
        JUST long enough to copy s_prev_vprintf +
        s_log_installed to locals, then releases the lock.
        Invokes the prior sink OUTSIDE the lock with an
        independent va_copy, and returns ITS byte count so
        callers keep the same byte-count contract. Formats the
        message OUTSIDE the lock with a second independent
        va_copy. On vsnprintf truncation rewrites the tail of
        the stack buffer with a clear '...[log truncated]\n'
        marker before it is copied to the ring, so a reader of
        the snapshot sees the loss instead of a silently
        cropped message. Reacquires s_log_lock ONLY for the
        bounded ring write. Both lock holds are bounded (two
        words on copy, ~<=256 bytes on ring write). The sink
        NEVER calls ESP_LOG and NEVER allocates. No no-wait
        take path — no diagnostic data is silently dropped.
      - log_ring_snapshot: reads s_log_installed under
        s_log_lock, then allocates a kLogRingSize heap buffer
        BEFORE the second critical section, memcpys the ring
        chronologically (head..end + 0..head when wrapped)
        UNDER the critical section, releases the critical
        section BEFORE returning to the caller, and hands the
        caller ownership of the heap buffer. Handles the
        allocation failure explicitly.
      - logs_get_handler: calls log_ring_snapshot, sets
        Content-Type text/plain; charset=utf-8 and
        Cache-Control: no-store, sends the buffer with
        httpd_resp_send, then free()s the buffer. An
        out-of-memory snapshot is surfaced as 503 with a small
        JSON error body; any other failure is 500.
    httpd_config max_uri_handlers is bumped from 6 to 7; the new
    kLogs register call rides the same atomic URI-registration
    failure branch that already retains s_server when httpd_stop
    also fails. No log text is redacted anywhere.

  * MODIFY main/app_main.cpp: call AliroLocalWebLogInit() as the
    very first line inside app_main() so the ring captures the
    earliest boot log lines.

Explicit non-goals for this patch: no log viewer UI, no OTA, no
WebSocket, no authentication, no settings backend changes.

